reteller is dipon gauche  and when i asked what he would stockpile  he said  pop secret home style microwave popcorn  please welcome  dupont hi  so my story starts near the end of my  time as a graduate student at yale  when i started a project exploring  whether this microscopic roundworm  called c  elegans could sense the color of  pigmented bacteria in its environment  now c elegans have neither eyes nor the  genes and proteins animals usually used  to see light let alone color  so these worms being able to sense  colors in their environment would be  like in a word unexpected and  plus it would be pretty difficult to  prove which is something i didn't fully  appreciate at the time  so for reference a reviewer of my  manuscript asked  did the authorist intend to study color  perception in the worm  the results of this manuscript are so  fascinating that one really wants to  hear some of the backstory of how the  authors found themselves in its pursuit  or in non-reviewer speak what did i just  read right so i started this project for  two main reasons  the first was pure unbridled curiosity  i had this idea and i just had to try it  out  the second is i wanted to do a project  all by myself  just to prove to myself that i could and  this second reason really crystallized  one day when i was at my bench observing  worms wiggling around on bacteria of  different colors  as one does when my advisor walked in  was like hey what are you up to which i  responded i won't tell you  in retrospect of course i was  unreasonably aggressive  but to my advisor's credit he just said  okay and he and he walked away and i  thought that was awesome because it  confirmed that i would be working  on my own but i guess i hadn't realized  just how alone i really was there are  zero papers  on this specific topic so i had no  guidebook  for how to proceed each experiment i was  designing more or less from scratch  and each result was a mini discovery but  what were the results telling me  did worms really behave differently with  respect to colorful bacteria  i was really struggling to conceptually  connect all these dots  and this was going on for nine months or  so until one day i announced to the lab  that we should go to a bar to hang out  i don't even drink but i figured that a  bar would be a great place to air my  grievances  about just how much my project sucked  not surprisingly complaining a hydrazium  didn't solve  any of my problems i was so frustrated  and out of just pure desperation i  decided  i had to solve everything right then  right there  so i'm back in my apartment this is like  11 or 12 at night now  and i pull up my white board which is a  must-have accessory for nerds  and i just start brainstorming when  suddenly it hits me  this whole time i've been thinking about  the worms exist in like a grayscale or a  black and white  world if you imagine a black and white  movie  there are no colors per se but you can  tell that something is a different color  because it appears brighter or darker  than something else  and so i was thinking that the worms  could sense colorful bacteria  if that bacteria made the worms world  darker  than wherever the world just came from  but taking a step back  my results were suggesting something  even crazier  worms weren't inferring color just  because their world was getting darker  but rather i think they were able to  discriminate between like  blue and orange so i was  so excited with that i couldn't even  really sleep that night because for the  first time  i had a coherent hypothesis and more  importantly  that hypothesis was testable which is by  far  my favorite kind of hypothesis so the  next morning i rushed to the art store  across the street from my apartment as  soon as it opened and i found exactly  what i was looking for  for ten dollars i bought a filter that  would change the color of the light  i'm back in lab i set up my experiments  with one set of plates illuminated  without the filter and another set of  plates illuminated  through this color filter i just bought  and the bacteria  are the same in both conditions and i  put worms on the bacteria  i give the worms one hour of privacy to  do their thing  and this whole time i'm super nervous  but i'm also so excited and then my  timer goes off  meaning one hour has elapsed and i go to  check on the plate  first i check on the worms on the worms  on the plates  that were illuminated with no filter  okay and on these plates the worms were  just hanging out  on the bacteria so cool all right  then i check on the plates where that  were illuminated using this color filter  and immediately i see that on these  plates the worms had actually  wandered off the bacteria i was blown  away by this discovery  again worms don't have eyes they don't  have the  usual ways in which animals see light  and yet here we are  based on their behavior it is clear that  these worms were somehow sensitive to  the color of light in their environment  that discovery was not only cool it was  also the breakthrough i  needed and that motivated me to keep  going so  fast forward four and a half years  because that's how science works  since that night and i'm still trying to  figure out how the worms sense colors  to the reviewer who asked did the  authors intend to study color perception  i can definitively say that yes i did  why because i just really thought they  might and i had to figure out if that  was right  this is one of the most uh mit-esque  stories i can imagine thank you so much  to pod i love that  we're talking about microscopic worms  and a 10 filter from the art shop over  the road  like that that really like was one of  the reasons i love  working for mit thank you so much japan  welcome our
 

